2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] with keyboard-driven navigation and cellular automaton rules. Here are my observations and
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] suggestions:
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Overall Structure*
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] - You have three main scripts (`traffic.ts`, `grid.ts`, `maze.ts`) sharing a common `terminal.ts`
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] utility.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The separation of concerns is good: keyboard I/O, display, and logic are in separate files.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The use of Unicode block and box‑drawing characters makes the output visually distinct.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Potential Issues & Improvements*
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. `boxIndex()` in `maze.ts` – This function uses many hard‑coded string comparisons to map
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3×3 block patterns to box‑drawing edges. It is brittle and will miss many valid patterns. Consider
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] replacing it with a logical test of the four cardinal neighbors (UDLR) or using a lookup table
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] generated from a small set of rules. The function `udlr()` already does this logic correctly (lines
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] ~178‑190), so `boxIndex()` may be redundant or should be refactored to use `udlr`.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Magic numbers – Throughout the code, numbers like `200`, `400`, `72`, `16`, etc., appear
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] without explanation. Defining named constants (e.g., `ACCELERATION`, `PAN_FACTOR`) would improve
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] readability and maintainability.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Keyboard handling – The `keyboardTask` function in `terminal.ts` sets raw mode but never
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] restores it if the program exits abnormally. A `try/finally` block or a cleanup handler would be
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] safer.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. `pump` array – In `grid.ts`, the pump values are integers representing accumulated
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] velocity. The fading logic (`fadePumps`) is hard to follow. Using a simpler exponential decay would
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] be clearer: `pump[i] = pump[i] * 0.9` (if using floating point) or `pump[i] -= Math.max(1, pump[i]
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] >> 3)` (as currently). The comment “fade=(integral>>3)” is misleading because `integral` is a
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] number, not necessarily an integer. Casting to `0` is unnecessary here.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 5. `gridWindow` bitmap rendering – The function samples bits at a quarter‑pixel resolution
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] using the `quads` string. This is clever but currently hard‑coded to a span of `gridBitmap.span*4`.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] If the bitmap dimensions change, the view must be recalculated. A more flexible approach would
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] compute the window parameters based on the actual bitmap size.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6. `replaceText` in `terminal.ts` – The “right‑to‑left” variant reverses the string. This
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] works, but be aware that it reverses the order of multi‑byte characters if any are present (Unicode
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] combining sequences, emoji). For the current character set (ASCII and single‑width Unicode), it is
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] safe.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 7. `maze.ts` drawing function – The `draw` function replaces runs of `hashChar` with
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] swizzled decorations, but the logic for `bed16`, `bed9`, `bed4`, `bed3` will replace any
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] contiguous run of that length, even if the run is part of a longer run. This can cause overlapping
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] replacements (e.g., a run of 16 will also be partially replaced by the 9‑char rule). Consider
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] processing runs from longest to shortest or using a different segmentation strategy.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 8. Edge wrapping – `wrapSample33` wraps around the grid, which may produce unexpected
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] connections at the boundary. `clampSample33` is more predictable for maze walls. Ensure you use the
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] correct one for each purpose.
2026-06-30 21:21:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
